  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct evidence – cftr → dysbiosis**  \n- In an intestinal‑epithelial‑specific Cftr knockout mouse, loss of Cftr alone was sufficient to produce a gut microbial shift characterized by enrichment of Proteobacteria, Enterobacteriaceae and other dysbiotic taxa【PMID: 37546931】 (animal evidence).  \n\n**Direct evidence – dysbiosis → inflammation**  \n- Whole‑body Cftr‑KO mice displayed gut dysbiosis (Enterobacteriaceae, Enterococcus spp.) that coincided with increased intestinal permeability and mucosal inflammation, evidenced by neutrophil, macrophage and T‑cell infiltration and elevated inflammatory mediators【PMID: 38554847】 (animal evidence).  \n\n**Association / overall mechanistic inference**  \n- The two studies together illustrate a sequential cascade: Cftr deficiency creates a dysbiotic intestinal environment, which in turn promotes barrier dysfunction and a pro‑inflammatory milieu. No additional experimental link (e.g., rescue of dysbiosis restoring inflammation) is provided in the cited records, so the complete mechanistic pathway remains inferred from the concatenation of the two hops.\n\n---\n\n### Graph‑derived candidate insights\n- **PATH 3a03360fb323a847 (GRAPH‑CONFIRMED):** cftr induces dysbiosis associated_with inflammation [PMID: 37546931, 38554847]  \n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:** Both animal studies supply direct evidence for each step of the cftr → dysbiosis → inflammation chain, and the graph confirms this two‑hop mechanistic connection."
  },
